{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he acted as a regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem during two separate periods. His life was defined by complex political maneuvering within the Crusader states while he faced constant threats from Muslim powers led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. Raymond inherited his title after his father died and he spent much of his early rule consolidating power in northern Syria. He became a central figure in the baronial opposition against Guy of Lusignan because he believed Guy lacked the skill to rule effectively during a time of crisis.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond was captured by Muslim forces in 1164 and remained a prisoner in Aleppo for roughly ten years before paying a massive ransom to secure his freedom. This long captivity shaped his worldview and made him more cautious regarding military engagements with neighboring Muslim emirates. He married Eschiva of Bures upon his release and this union gave him control over the Principality of Galilee which increased his influence significantly within the kingdom. His experience in prison taught him the value of diplomacy over rash military action and he often advocated for truces with Muslim leaders to preserve the fragile Crusader states.\n\n*Regency and Political Conflict*\nHe first served as regent for Baldwin IV when the young king was still a minor and he managed the realm with considerable skill during this initial period. Tensions arose later when Baldwin IV grew older and factions formed around Raymond and the court party supporting Guy of Lusignan. Raymond opposed the marriage between Sibylla and Guy because he feared their combined rule would lead to disaster for the kingdom. He attempted to assert his own authority by convening a high court and seeking support from other nobles who shared his concerns about the direction of the monarchy under Guy.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death*\nRaymond eventually reconciled with Guy of Lusignan shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 although he remained skeptical of the strategy employed by the king. During the battle itself Raymond commanded a wing of the army but he broke through the Muslim encirclement and escaped while most of the Christian forces were annihilated or captured. This decision to flee drew criticism from some contemporaries yet it allowed him to survive when nearly all other major leaders perished or fell into captivity. He retreated to Tripoli after the defeat and died later that same year reportedly from pleurisy or perhaps despair over the collapse of the kingdom he had tried to save. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and removed a key stabilizing force from the region just as Saladin began his conquest of the remaining Crusader holdings.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0017609595}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0013540012}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.039401766}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -5.448013e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -6.5565323e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.3644326e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.861027e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0